---
// firma2-project-atlas — the project record with the MAP AS THE PAGE. Same
// record, same sections, same components; the difference is which one is the
// ground and which ones are laid on it.
//
// WHAT THIS IS AN ALTERNATIVE TO. The project detail page reads the record as a
// DOCUMENT: a column of sections in the order a reader judges delivery, with the
// map last because "where" is not the question that page owes an answer to. That
// argument is written out at length in that page's own header and it is still
// correct for the reader it was written for — someone checking whether a project
// is on track. This is the same record for the reader who arrives with the other
// question: a field or program lead who is looking at PLACE first, and wants the
// numbers as annotations on it rather than the other way round. Neither ordering
// is a fix for the other; they are two readings, and the record is one.
//
// SO THE MAP IS NOT A SECTION HERE. It is the pane, edge to edge and top to
// bottom, and every other part of the record floats on it: the index card on the
// leading edge, the tracked sections on the trailing edge. Nothing is laid out
// AROUND the map, which is the property that makes the last control work — the
// panels can all leave and there is no gap where they were, because they never
// took any room. That is why they are floating panels and not a second and third
// layout track: a docked column that collapses re-flows the map every time it
// moves, and a map that re-frames on every click is the thing this screen most
// has to avoid.
//
// THE ONE CONTROL. "Hide details" takes the record, the sections AND the page
// header band off the screen in one click, and puts them back in the next. It is
// one toggle rather than a collapse affordance per panel because the state a
// reader actually wants is a MODE — "let me look at this" — and three separate
// two-state controls make them ask for it three times. The map's own chrome
// stays: the section title, Add area and the legend are the MAP, not details
// about the project, and the legend is also this surface's text alternative —
// the one part of the screen a reader who cannot see the shapes is reading. A
// "clear everything" that took the legend would be clearing the content.
//
// THE TOOLBAR NEVER COLLAPSES, for the reason every full-screen mode has to be
// escapable by the affordance that entered it. It keeps the same corner in both
// states so the way back is where the way in was, and Escape exits as well.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: resize the map. Hiding the panels reveals map that was
// already drawn under them. The one thing that does change size is the pane
// itself when the header band goes, and the map is told about that — see the
// reflow in the script, and firma2-project-map's listener for why an event is
// the right seam between two islands that share no module.
//
// bcn-lego-checked: nothing in the catalog is a map workspace, and this file
// hand-rolls no primitive — it is a manifest. The surface is
// firma2-project-map (variant="canvas"); the three floating regions are
// firma2-map-panel, the shared component written for exactly this; the record's
// tabs are firma2-project-rail and the four sections are the same
// firma2-project-* components the document reading of this page renders, passed
// the same props. The toggle is esa-button. The only CSS here is the pane's own
// geometry — the height it takes from the fold, the bleed through the content
// pane's padding, and the two reservations it publishes to the map so the map's
// chrome clears the panels. Walked the catalog for a full-bleed pane primitive
// (esa-container is a max-width content column; `.frame` in layouts.css is a
// ratio-locked media box, and this is the opposite — a box whose ratio is
// whatever the window is). Beacon is not cloned on this machine.
import Firma2MapPanel from '../shared/firma2-map-panel.astro';
import Firma2ProjectMap from './firma2-project-map.astro';
import Firma2ProjectRail from './firma2-project-rail.astro';
import Firma2ProjectMeasures from './firma2-project-measures.astro';
import Firma2ProjectExpenditures from './firma2-project-expenditures.astro';
import Firma2ProjectFunding from './firma2-project-funding.astro';
import Firma2ProjectTimeline from './firma2-project-timeline.astro';
import EsaButton from '@esa/ecology/esa-button.astro';
import { projectSlug } from '../../data/firma2-projects';
import type { ProjectDetail } from '../../data/firma2-project-detail';

interface Props {
  /** The whole record. Every panel takes its own slice of it, the same slices
   *  the document reading of this page passes to the same components. */
  detail: ProjectDetail;
}

const { detail } = Astro.props;
---
<!-- `data-chrome` IS THE MODE, and it is on the outermost element because all
     three things it governs are in different places: the band is a child here,
     the panels are two components down, and the map's plate reservation is a
     custom property that has to inherit past both. One attribute at the top is
     the only place all three can be reached from. -->
<div class="firma2-project-atlas" data-chrome="shown">
  <!-- THE BAND IS INSIDE THE ATLAS, not above it on the page, and that is the
       whole reason this component takes a slot instead of the page rendering the
       header itself. "Hide details" has to reach it — the band is the last thing
       between the map and the top of the window — and a component cannot hide a
       sibling it does not contain without reaching through a page's stylesheet
       to do it. Slotted, it is simply a child that gets displayed or not.

       It keeps firma2-page-header's own bleed and its own sticky rule, both of
       which are inert here and harmless: nothing scrolls on this page, so a
       sticky box with nowhere to travel is just a box. -->
  <div class="firma2-project-atlas__band">
    <slot name="header" />
  </div>

  <!-- THE STAGE IS THE POSITIONED BOX EVERYTHING ELSE IS PLACED AGAINST, and it
       has to be exactly one box for that to work: the map fills it, the three
       panels are absolute against it, and the two reservations below are custom
       properties that inherit from it down into the map. -->
  <div class="firma2-project-atlas__stage">
    <Firma2ProjectMap detail={detail} variant="canvas" />

    <!-- FIRST OF THE THREE IN THE DOM, AND LAST OF THEM ON THE SCREEN'S
         READING ORDER — which is the right way round. It is absolutely
         positioned, so source order costs the layout nothing, and it buys a
         keyboard reader the control that governs the whole screen a couple of
         Tabs after the header instead of past two panels of record. The one
         affordance that can clear the page should not be behind everything the
         page is about.

         ON A PLATE AND NOT ON THE TILES: esa-button's `chrome`
         variant is transparent until hovered and takes its focus ring from
         currentColor — it is built for a dark app bar, and on a basemap it would
         be a label lying on a road. The plate is what gives it a ground, and it
         is the same plate the map draws under its own Add area button, from the
         same component, so the two read as one toolbar in two corners.

         `width: auto` because a toolbar is as wide as its control. No `label` on
         the panel: a complementary landmark per button makes the landmark list
         longer without making it more useful, and the button names itself.

         NO data-atlas-collapsible — see the module header. -->
    <Firma2MapPanel side="end" width="auto" gap="xs" class="firma2-project-atlas__tools">
      <!-- THE LABEL FLIPS. A toggle that keeps one label and reports its state
           through aria-pressed alone is correct markup and the wrong copy here:
           the state it is reporting is that most of the screen has just gone,
           and the reader's next question is how to get it back. Naming the
           action in both directions answers that without their having to work
           out that this is the control that did it. aria-pressed rides along
           regardless — it is what a screen reader announces, before a reader has
           to infer anything from a label that changed under them.

           BOTH LABELS ARE TWELVE CHARACTERS, which is not a coincidence worth
           losing: a toolbar plate is sized to its content, and a control that
           changed width on click would move the one affordance still on screen.

           NO ICON, and that is a deletion rather than an omission. `expand` was
           right for the outbound half — more map — and there is no glyph in the
           hub's registry that is its honest inverse, so the pair would have been
           one accurate icon and one decorative one. design-principles: if an
           element carries no information, cut it. The label is unambiguous on
           its own and the plate is what makes it legible over tiles. -->
      <EsaButton variant="chrome" size="md" data-atlas-toggle aria-pressed="false">
        Hide details
      </EsaButton>
    </Firma2MapPanel>

    <!-- THE RECORD, FLOATING. Identical content and identical component to the
         rail on the document reading of this page — three tabs, Key facts
         leading — because it is answering the identical question there and here:
         what IS this project, who do I call about it, what has been said about
         it. Only the surface it sits on changed.

         Tabs earn their keep harder on this screen than on that one. There, the
         rail was competing with a scroll; here it is competing with the MAP, and
         every row of record on screen is a row of the thing the reader came for
         covered up. Three peers behind one 22rem column is the smallest this
         content comes. -->
    <Firma2MapPanel side="start" label="Project record" data-atlas-collapsible>
      <Firma2ProjectRail detail={detail} />
    </Firma2MapPanel>

    <!-- THE TRACKED SECTIONS, IN THE ORDER THEY ALWAYS RUN: what was built, what
         it cost, who paid for it, when it happened. The order is not this
         screen's decision to make — it is the record's, and re-ordering it here
         would mean the same project reads two ways depending on which page you
         opened.

         `surface="bare"`: each of these four is already an esa-card, a white box
         with an edge. A plate behind them would put four boxes inside a fifth.

         It starts below the toolbar rather than at the top of the surface. The
         alternative was to move the toolbar, and every other corner is spoken
         for — the record has the leading top, the map's legend the leading foot,
         Leaflet's attribution the trailing foot. -->
    <Firma2MapPanel
      side="end"
      label="Progress and funding"
      surface="bare"
      style="--firma2-map-panel-inset-block-start: var(--firma2-atlas-sections-top); --firma2-map-panel-width: var(--firma2-atlas-sections-width)"
      data-atlas-collapsible
    >
      <Firma2ProjectMeasures measures={detail.measures} projectSlug={projectSlug(detail.project)} />
      <Firma2ProjectExpenditures
        expenditures={detail.expenditures}
        estimatedTotalCost={detail.project.estimatedTotalCost}
      />
      <Firma2ProjectFunding
        funding={detail.funding}
        estimatedTotalCost={detail.project.estimatedTotalCost}
      />
      <Firma2ProjectTimeline milestones={detail.milestones} />
    </Firma2MapPanel>

  </div>
</div>

<style>
  /* ---- THE PANE ------------------------------------------------------------
     THIS SCREEN DOES NOT SCROLL, and everything below is in service of that.
     The map is the page and a map you have to scroll to see the bottom of is a
     map in a box. So the atlas is exactly the height of what is left of the
     window under the app bar, and the stage takes whatever the band does not.

     THE HEIGHT IS MEASURED, NOT COMPUTED. The fallback below is a real number
     and it is wrong on most windows: what sits above this element is the app
     bar (content-sized, and the shell exposes no token for it) plus the content
     pane's padding, and neither is knowable from CSS here. The script measures
     the element's own distance from the top of the viewport and publishes it,
     which is right by construction at every width and on every resize — and,
     unlike a chain of subtractions, cannot drift when something upstream
     changes its padding.

     This is the same problem firma2-project-map solves in `section` variant by
     subtracting --firma2-band-height, and the reason that map hands the
     arithmetic over in `canvas` variant: doing it in both places is two answers
     that agree until they do not. */
  .firma2-project-atlas {
    --_bleed: var(--spacing-500, 1.5rem);
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    block-size: var(--firma2-atlas-height, calc(100dvh - 10rem));

    /* THE BLOCK-END BLEED IS ON THE PANE, NOT ON THE STAGE, and the difference
       is measurable rather than stylistic — it was on the stage first and cost
       this screen 24px of document scroll.
       A negative margin does not shrink the box it is on; it pulls what comes
       AFTER it up. On the stage, "after it" is the end of this element, whose
       height is fixed — so nothing moved, and the content pane still added its
       own bottom padding under a full-height pane, which is exactly one step of
       overflow. Here, "after it" is the pane's bottom padding, which is the
       thing that had to move. The pane's padding is given back, the document
       ends on the fold, and the map reaches the bottom of the window.
       The inline bleed stays on the stage — see the note there for why it cannot
       be on this element. */
    margin-block-end: calc(var(--_bleed) * -1);
  }

  /* The band brings its own full-bleed and its own sticky rule from
     firma2-page-header; this wrapper only exists so the mode has something to
     hide. `flex: none` so it keeps its measured height and the stage takes the
     rest — without it a flex child with no basis would share the column. */
  .firma2-project-atlas__band {
    flex: none;
  }

  .firma2-project-atlas[data-chrome='hidden'] .firma2-project-atlas__band {
    display: none;
  }

  /* ---- THE STAGE -----------------------------------------------------------
     THE BOX THE MAP FILLS AND THE PANELS ARE PLACED AGAINST. `position:
     relative` is what makes the panels' `position: absolute` mean "this
     surface"; `min-block-size: 0` is what lets a flex child with a tall
     scrolling panel inside it actually shrink to its share of the column
     instead of being sized by its content.

     THE INLINE BLEED IS HERE AND NOT ON THE ATLAS, and that is not a stylistic
     choice: firma2-page-header ALREADY bleeds a step past its column on both
     inline edges, and the band is a child of the atlas. A negative inline margin
     up there would compound with the band's own and push it two steps out, past
     the pane and under the app's nav rail. The band keeps its bleed; this one
     reaches through the pane's padding for everything below it.

     The BLOCK-end bleed is the atlas's, for the opposite reason — see the note
     there. Same value on both, from the same token, because it is the same one
     literal: esa-app-shell's `padding: var(--spacing-500)` on its content
     region, which the shell does not expose as a hook (the /request-lego for
     `--app-shell-content-padding` is filed in firma2-page-header's own note, and
     would retire the restatement in all three files at once).

     `isolation: isolate` even though the map opens a stacking context of its
     own. That one contains Leaflet; this one contains the panels, which are
     positioned siblings of it. Without it the two are ordered against whatever
     the page's ancestors happen to have created. */
  .firma2-project-atlas__stage {
    /* WHAT THE MAP HAS TO CLEAR, PUBLISHED AS A CUSTOM PROPERTY BECAUSE THAT IS
       THE ONLY THING THAT INHERITS. firma2-project-map draws its own chrome at
       the leading edge — the section title and Add area at the top, the legend
       at the foot — and the record panel is sitting on both of those corners.
       The map exposes the leading inset as a knob for exactly this; the atlas is
       the only component that knows how wide the panel is, so the arithmetic
       lives here. One panel width, two gutters: the panel's own inset from the
       edge, and a second so the plates are not touching it.

       --firma2-atlas-panel-width is declared once and read twice for the same
       reason: a panel that got wider and a reservation that did not would put a
       plate under it, which is the one failure a map-as-backdrop has that a
       map-in-a-card does not. */
    --firma2-atlas-panel-width: 22rem;

    /* THE TWO PANELS ARE NOT THE SAME WIDTH, and the difference is the brief's
       rail test doing its job rather than an inconsistency. 22rem is what the
       record's cards were written for and measure at everywhere in this spoke —
       they are scalars, one label over one value, and they read fine in a narrow
       column. The tracked sections are the content that was taken OUT of the
       rail for failing that test, and the funding table is the specific reason:
       four columns, two of them organization names, with a measured minimum of
       441px. At 22rem it does not overflow gracefully, it draws a horizontal
       scrollbar inside a card — which is exactly the failure that moved it back
       to the main column on the document reading of this record.
       28rem is the first step that clears 441px plus esa-card's own padding,
       measured in the browser rather than reasoned about. Expenditures' chart
       clears at 24rem and Measures and Milestones at 22rem, so funding is the
       binding constraint and this number is its. */
    --firma2-atlas-sections-width: 28rem;

    --firma2-atlas-gutter: var(--spacing-400, 1rem);
    --firma2-map-plate-inset-inline-start: calc(
      var(--firma2-atlas-gutter) * 2 + var(--firma2-atlas-panel-width)
    );

    /* AND THE PANELS TAKE THEIR WIDTH FROM THE SAME DECLARATION. firma2-map-panel
       reads this off its surface rather than carrying a width of its own,
       precisely so the panel and the reservation above cannot be changed
       independently. The toolbar is the exception and says so where it is
       rendered: it passes `width="auto"`, which is an inline style and outranks
       this. */
    --firma2-map-panel-width: var(--firma2-atlas-panel-width);

    /* WHERE THE SECTIONS PANEL STARTS: under the toolbar. The value is written
       by the script, off the toolbar's RENDERED height, and the fallback here is
       what it comes out at today — one gutter above the toolbar, 75px of
       toolbar, one gutter below.

       IT IS MEASURED BECAUSE IT WAS COMPUTED FIRST AND THE ARITHMETIC WAS WRONG.
       The computed version added the app's 40px control rung to the plate's
       padding and its two hairlines and got 66px for a box that renders at 75:
       esa-button's md rung is its own (padding plus a label composite's line
       box), not the 40px AppLayout pins the FIELD controls to, and there is no
       token for either. Nine pixels is not a rounding error when it is the gap
       between two floating panels. The toolbar is a real box on the page and the
       browser already knows how tall it is; asking it cannot be wrong, and does
       not go stale when the label or the control size changes. */
    --firma2-atlas-sections-top: 107px;

    position: relative;
    flex: 1;
    min-block-size: 0;
    margin-inline: calc(var(--_bleed) * -1);
    isolation: isolate;
  }

  /* THE PLATES COME BACK TO THE EDGE WHEN THE PANEL COVERING THEM LEAVES. One
     declaration, and it is the reason the map took a knob instead of a constant:
     with the record gone there is nothing at the leading edge, and a title plate
     still standing 24rem in would read as an alignment bug. firma2-project-map
     transitions the inset, so the plates travel rather than jump. */
  .firma2-project-atlas[data-chrome='hidden'] .firma2-project-atlas__stage {
    --firma2-map-plate-inset-inline-start: var(--firma2-atlas-gutter);

    /* AND THE MAP TAKES THE BAND'S ROOM. The band bleeds a step ABOVE this
       element (firma2-page-header's own `margin-block-start`), so hiding it
       leaves that step of page canvas between the app bar and the map. Reaching
       back through it puts the map against the bar, which is what "only see the
       map" has to mean at the top edge as much as at the other three. Flex
       counts margins in its free-space arithmetic, so the stage grows by exactly
       what it reaches back through — the height is not overrun. */
    margin-block-start: calc(var(--_bleed) * -1);
  }

  /* The toolbar's own corner: the trailing top, one gutter in, which is the same
     step every panel and plate on this surface keeps. It is the one region that
     is in the same place in both modes. */
  .firma2-project-atlas__tools {
    --firma2-map-panel-inset-block-start: var(--firma2-atlas-gutter);
  }

  /* THE PANELS NARROW BEFORE THEY MEET IN THE MIDDLE. Two 22rem columns and
     three gutters need 47rem before a single tile of map shows between them, and
     a window that narrow closes the gap while the app's nav is still taking its
     own 18rem out of the same width — at which point the record and the sections
     are not floating ON anything, they are a two-column layout with a texture
     behind it.

     18rem is the same floor `.sidebar` takes as its own default track and holds
     every row in the record's Key facts on one line; 64rem is the width
     firma2-record-rail unpins itself at, for the same reason and with the same
     caveat written out there — it is a viewport query standing in for a
     container one, because the pane's width also depends on whether the app's
     nav is collapsed and a container query needs `container-type` on a box this
     component does not own.

     ONE DECLARATION MOVES THE RECORD PANEL AND THE MAP'S RESERVATION FOR IT,
     which is the whole point of their sharing a name: they cannot come out of
     step at a breakpoint.

     THIS IS THE SECOND HALF OF A PAIR: the script opens the page collapsed at
     the same breakpoint, so these widths are what the panels come back at when
     the reader asks for them, not what they land on. See the matchMedia at the
     foot of the script for why that is one state rather than a third mode.

     WHAT NARROWING COSTS, STATED RATHER THAN HIDDEN: at 22rem the sections panel
     is back under the funding table's 441px minimum, so that one card scrolls
     horizontally inside itself, and the two panels are close enough to cover the
     map's own title plate. Both are real regressions and both are the price of
     showing a whole record at this width; the alternatives are a layout that
     decides for the reader which half of their record to drop, or panels wide
     enough to overlap each other. The reader who wants neither has the toolbar,
     and the document reading of the same record one crumb away — that page is
     built for a column this narrow and this one is not. */
  @media (max-width: 64rem) {
    .firma2-project-atlas__stage {
      --firma2-atlas-panel-width: 18rem;
      --firma2-atlas-sections-width: 22rem;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // ── THE MODE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  //
  // One attribute drives all of it (`data-chrome` on the atlas), and this script
  // owns exactly three things beyond flipping it: the two panels' own collapsed
  // attribute, the toggle's label and pressed state, and telling the map its box
  // changed. Everything visual is a stylesheet rule keyed on those attributes —
  // no styles are written from here, and no markup is authored.

  const LABEL = { hide: 'Hide details', show: 'Show details' } as const;

  function initAtlas(atlas: HTMLElement): void {
    // esa-button FORWARDS data-* TO THE NATIVE CONTROL and keeps `class` on its
    // styled wrapper — documented in the lego, and the reason these are two
    // lookups rather than one. `toggle` is the real <button>: it takes the
    // click, the accessible name and aria-pressed. `chrome` is the <span> the
    // stylesheet's variant rules are written against, so the pressed LOOK has to
    // go there. Reaching for either one alone silently half-works.
    const toggle = atlas.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('button[data-atlas-toggle]');
    const chrome = toggle?.closest<HTMLElement>('.esa-button');
    const stage = atlas.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.firma2-project-atlas__stage');
    const map = atlas.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.firma2-project-map');
    const panels = atlas.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-atlas-collapsible]');
    if (!toggle || !stage) return;

    // THE HEIGHT, MEASURED. See the note on the pane in the stylesheet: what sits
    // above this element is the app bar plus the content pane's padding, neither
    // of which CSS here can read.
    //
    // ZEROED BEFORE MEASURING, and that is the line that makes this correct
    // rather than nearly correct. getBoundingClientRect().top is relative to the
    // VIEWPORT, so it is only the layout position while nothing has scrolled —
    // and the thing most likely to make this page scroll is this element being a
    // few pixels too tall from the previous measurement. Collapsing it first
    // guarantees the document is shorter than the window at the moment the
    // reading is taken. The write forces the layout; the read is then exact.
    const sizePane = (): void => {
      atlas.style.setProperty('--firma2-atlas-height', '0px');
      const top = atlas.getBoundingClientRect().top;
      atlas.style.setProperty('--firma2-atlas-height', `${Math.round(window.innerHeight - top)}px`);

      // AND WHERE THE SECTIONS PANEL CLEARS THE TOOLBAR. Measured for the reason
      // written out in the stylesheet: the computed form got this 9px wrong,
      // because esa-button's md height is padding plus a type composite's line
      // box and no token names it. Both insets come from the same declaration
      // the toolbar itself uses, so the gap under it is the gap beside it.
      const tools = stage.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.firma2-project-atlas__tools');
      if (tools) {
        const box = tools.getBoundingClientRect();
        // The gutter is not restated either — it is read as the distance the
        // toolbar already keeps from the top of the surface, which is the same
        // step every panel and plate on this map takes. So the space UNDER the
        // toolbar is the space ABOVE it, by construction rather than by two
        // declarations agreeing.
        const gutter = Math.round(box.top - stage.getBoundingClientRect().top);
        stage.style.setProperty(
          '--firma2-atlas-sections-top',
          `${gutter * 2 + Math.round(box.height)}px`,
        );
      }
    };

    // LEAFLET CACHES ITS CONTAINER'S SIZE. Hiding the band gives the map another
    // band's worth of height and Leaflet paints tiles for the size it last knew,
    // so it has to be told — and once it is being told, it may as well re-frame:
    // the shapes were fitted into the room LEFT OVER by the panels, and with the
    // panels gone that framing is off-centre by two panel widths.
    //
    // The reservation is measured off the panels themselves rather than restated
    // here. They are the boxes that do the covering, and their width is a CSS
    // value this file does not own.
    const reflow = (): void => {
      if (!map) return;

      // COLLAPSED, THE MAP GOES BACK TO ITS OWN RESERVATION — an event with no
      // detail, which is what firma2-project-map's defaults are for. There is
      // nothing left on the surface but the map's own plates, and those are the
      // boxes it already fits itself around. Restating their numbers here would
      // be a second copy that goes stale on the commit that moves them.
      if (atlas.dataset.chrome === 'hidden') {
        map.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent('firma2-map:reflow'));
        return;
      }

      // SHOWN, THE RESERVATION IS MEASURED OFF THE PANELS THEMSELVES. Not
      // computed from the width token: what has to be cleared is the panel's far
      // edge plus the gutter it keeps from the surface, and both of those are
      // CSS this file does not own — the panel picks its own inset, the media
      // query above moves its width, and `max-inline-size` can override the lot
      // on a narrow window. The DOM already holds the answer; asking it is one
      // line and cannot drift.
      const surface = stage.getBoundingClientRect();
      let start = 0;
      let end = 0;
      panels.forEach((panel) => {
        const box = panel.getBoundingClientRect();
        if (panel.dataset.side === 'start') {
          // The panel's far edge, plus its own inset again so the shapes are not
          // fitted flush against it.
          start = Math.max(start, Math.round(box.right - surface.left + (box.left - surface.left)));
        } else {
          end = Math.max(end, Math.round(surface.right - box.left + (surface.right - box.right)));
        }
      });

      // The block axis stays the map's own: its title plate takes the top of the
      // surface and its legend the foot, in both modes, and the panels are
      // inside those bounds. The two numbers are the map's own defaults, restated
      // only because Leaflet's asymmetric form takes the corners as a pair and
      // this call is overriding the inline half of each.
      map.dispatchEvent(
        new CustomEvent('firma2-map:reflow', {
          detail: { paddingTopLeft: [start, 96], paddingBottomRight: [end, 132] },
        }),
      );
    };

    const setMode = (hidden: boolean): void => {
      // FOCUS CANNOT BE LEFT INSIDE SOMETHING THAT IS ABOUT TO BE HIDDEN. The
      // panels leave with `visibility: hidden`, which takes them out of the tab
      // order and out of the accessibility tree — and a keyboard reader who was
      // on a tab or an Add button in one of them would be left focused on a box
      // that no longer exists, with the next Tab starting again from the top of
      // the document. The toggle is where they should be: it is the control that
      // just acted, and it is the way back.
      if (hidden && document.activeElement instanceof HTMLElement) {
        const inside = Array.from(panels).some((panel) => panel.contains(document.activeElement));
        if (inside) toggle.focus();
      }

      atlas.dataset.chrome = hidden ? 'hidden' : 'shown';
      panels.forEach((panel) => panel.toggleAttribute('data-collapsed', hidden));

      // aria-pressed is what a screen reader announces; .esa-button--active is
      // the lego's OWN class for a pressed toggle, so the look comes from the
      // design system rather than from a colour written here.
      toggle.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(hidden));
      const text = toggle.querySelector('.esa-button__label') ?? toggle;
      text.textContent = hidden ? LABEL.show : LABEL.hide;
      chrome?.classList.toggle('esa-button--active', hidden);

      // Order matters. The band's display change has to be in the layout before
      // the map is asked how big it is, and one frame is what guarantees that.
      requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        sizePane();
        reflow();
      });
    };

    toggle.addEventListener('click', () => setMode(atlas.dataset.chrome !== 'hidden'));

    // EVERY FULL-SCREEN MODE IS ESCAPABLE BY Escape. It is the key a reader
    // presses without being told, and the alternative — hunting for the one
    // control still on screen — is the failure mode of every kiosk view.
    // Focus goes back to the toggle so a keyboard reader is not dropped on the
    // map with no idea what just happened.
    document.addEventListener('keydown', (event: KeyboardEvent) => {
      if (event.key !== 'Escape' || atlas.dataset.chrome !== 'hidden') return;
      setMode(false);
      toggle.focus();
    });

    sizePane();

    // A WINDOW TOO NARROW FOR THIS LAYOUT OPENS IN THE MODE THAT FITS IT.
    // Two panels and the gutters between them need about 47rem of pane, and the
    // pane is the window less the app's nav rail — so under 64rem they meet in
    // the middle, cover the map's own title plate, and leave a strip of basemap
    // too thin to read a shape in. The layout has not degraded at that point, it
    // has stopped being a map screen.
    //
    // So it starts collapsed there, which is the same state the toggle produces
    // and the same one click out of it. That is deliberately NOT a third mode
    // with rules of its own: a reader gets a full-screen map and a labelled
    // control that says "Show details", and pressing it gives them everything —
    // panels overlapping the map's plates, which is what it costs at that width
    // and is at least an honest stacking order rather than a broken one.
    //
    // Matched ONCE, at load, and never re-run. It is the opening state, not a
    // responsive rule: a reader who has asked for their record and then resized
    // the window has said what they want, and taking it away again would be the
    // layout overruling them.
    if (window.matchMedia('(max-width: 64rem)').matches) setMode(true);

    // THE MAP FRAMES ITSELF BEFORE IT KNOWS THE PANELS ARE THERE, and this is
    // the correction. Its Leaflet module waits on a CDN script, so it finishes
    // long after this one and its first fit reserves only its own plates — which
    // on this screen puts a work area under the sections panel. It announces
    // itself when it is up; the answer is the same reflow a click sends, with
    // the panels' measured boxes in it. Listening rather than polling, and no
    // ordering assumption in either direction: if this handler is attached after
    // the map is up the click still corrects it, and if the map never loads
    // nothing is waiting on it.
    map?.addEventListener('firma2-map:ready', reflow);

    window.addEventListener('resize', () => {
      sizePane();
      reflow();
    });
  }

  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.firma2-project-atlas').forEach(initAtlas);
</script>
